WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:11.370
In this video, we're going to discuss authorship.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:15.020
The main advice on authorship is to think
about the authorship before you write the

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.180
paper.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.170
Especially, if you're the person drafting
the paper.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.960
Think about who is going to get authorship.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.370
Think about what the order will be.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:29.890
Go through that with the team so that everybody
is on the same page and nobody feels slighted

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:31.570
later on.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:36.160
There are two questions that you have to answer
with authorship.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.400
First of all, who gets authorship?

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.320
Who are you going to include as an author
on your paper?

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.950
According to the International Committee of
Medical Journal Editors recommendation, authorship

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:52.489
should be based on the following 4 criteria:

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:53.969
1.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:59.170
Substantial contributions to the conception
or design of the work; or the acquisition,

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:04.239
analysis, or interpretation of data for the
work; AND

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:05.600
1.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.220
Drafting the work or revising it critically
for important intellectual content; AND

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.220
2.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.280
Final approval of the version to be published;
AND

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.280
2.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.170
Agreement to be accountable for all aspects
of the work in ensuring that questions related

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.830
to the accuracy or integrity of any part of
the work are appropriately investigated and

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.540
resolved.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.299
The main criterion here is that if you are
an author listed on the paper, then you are

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:38.080
taking public responsibility for its content.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.900
That's a high bar.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.670
It means that if later, the paper is found
to have a problem.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.880
If it’s found to have fraud, if it’s retracted.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.850
You are responsible.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.640
You are liable for those transgressions.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.340
And that is going to reflect badly on you.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:00.590
So, if you are not the primary author of the
paper, before you accept co-authorship, ask

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:05.520
yourself the question as to whether you want
to take that level of responsibility.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.599
Do you really understand the ins and outs
of the paper well enough?

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:13.540
You have to decide whether you have been sufficiently
involved with its content to feel that you

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.220
can take public responsibility.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.340
If you're the one writing the paper and trying
to decide on authors, keep in mind that most

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:25.079
people don't want to be included on your paper
unless they have been fully involved.

00:02:25.079 --> 00:02:28.680
You can always put them in the acknowledgment
section if you think that they have assisted

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:36.319
in part of the study but not sufficiently
involved to qualify them to be co-author.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:40.639
There is also the issue of order, what order
with the authors appear in?

00:02:40.639 --> 00:02:44.980
In general, order implies the authors' relative
contributions.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.970
The first author is usually the person who
wrote up the draft of the paper, might have

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:54.890
been the person who collected all the data,
often a graduate student, or a junior person.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:59.629
And the last position is usually the senior
author, the head of the lab or research team,

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:03.059
or the senior person who oversaw the research.

00:03:03.059 --> 00:03:06.150
That's a position of stature as well.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.089
Sometimes you'll have dual first authors,
if maybe two graduate students worked on it

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.129
together equally.

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:16.029
In between the first and last positions, you
should put everyone else in their order of

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:18.180
their contributions.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.219
If it's not clear if the rest of the authors
contributed equally without much differentiation,

00:03:23.219 --> 00:03:28.629
you could consider putting them in alphabetical
order or even reverse alphabetical order.

00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:33.260
And sometimes you get these large working
groups with lots and lots of people in international

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.170
collaborations.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:39.209
Those large working groups can be cited as
a group because they often comprise hundreds

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:43.319
of authors.

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:46.339
Papers also have an acknowledgements section.

00:03:46.339 --> 00:03:49.599
That's where you are going to cite any funding
sources.

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:53.779
If you're a graduate student who is on a certain
fellowship, make sure you cite your funding

00:03:53.779 --> 00:03:55.540
source there.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.930
If it was a grant funded study, you're going
to mention the grant there.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:04.079
This is also a good place to acknowledge people
who you don't feel merit authorship, and who

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:08.620
probably don't want to be authors on the paper,
but maybe they gave you some materials, or

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.809
they offered some statistical consulting or
some other advice.

00:04:12.809 --> 00:04:19.889
It's a way to acknowledge those people without
making them take on the role of author.

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:24.639
There is also an ethical issues of ghost authorship
and guest authorship.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:28.699
This is a hot topic particularly in the medical
literature.

00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:32.650
It's been written about in the New York Times
and its had some pretty profound influence

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.030
on the medical literature.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.590
It's important that you are aware of these
practices.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.580
Obviously, they are to be avoided when it
comes to authorship.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:48.050
A ghost author is a professional writer who
usually a company hires to draft a manuscript.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.840
At the end of the day the writer is not listed
as an author on the manuscript though they

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.370
may have written the whole paper.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.150
Hence the term, ghost authorship.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:02.650
There's nothing wrong with professional writers
being involved in the writing process.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:06.699
Scientists can certainly use the help of writers,
but it's the lack of transparency that's the

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:09.220
problem here.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:15.090
The related type of authorship is guest authorship,
or sometimes called honorary authorship.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.969
This is when a company does a study, analyzes
the data, draws their conclusions, drafts

00:05:19.969 --> 00:05:25.010
the paper, then they call or email an academic
researcher at a prestigious university and

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:27.430
invite them to be an author.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.629
That academic researcher certainly can't take
public responsibility for the study.

00:05:32.629 --> 00:05:36.710
But they're often willing to lend their name
because drug company studies are often large

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:41.900
high quality clinical trials that get published
in great journals, and this goes on the academics

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:43.320
resume.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.760
The drug company meanwhile gets to bolster
their papers credibility by leading researchers

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.180
to believe that an outside independent unbiased
expert was a driving force in the study.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:58.180
Obviously, this is misleading if in fact the
academic was only minimally involved and is

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:03.380
simply lending their name, and their institution's
name to the paper.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:08.930
It's unclear how common these types of unethical
authorship practices are in the literature.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:13.470
But a study in the British Medical Journal
in 2011 that surveyed top medical journals

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:19.389
found evidence of honorary or guest authorship
in 17.6% of papers.

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:23.900
And evidence of ghost authorship in 7.6% of
papers.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:30.780
So at least in 2011, this practice was fairly
pervasive.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.660
If you're interested in learning more about
this, the medical community learned a lot

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:39.290
about this practice because of litigation
surrounding the drug Vioxx.

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:43.469
Merck was being sued over the drug Vioxx,
which was to found to increase the risk of

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:45.550
heart attacks.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:50.499
Because of the court trials, internal company
documents entered the public domain.

00:06:50.499 --> 00:06:55.379
And there's a fascinating article in JAMA
from 2008, where some researchers systematically

00:06:55.379 --> 00:07:02.180
reviewed 250 court documents related to published
clinical trial and published review papers.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.340
What they found was shocking.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:09.210
The researchers reviewed emails and other
documents related to 24 clinical trial papers

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.800
that Merck was involved with.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.189
Here's what they found:

00:07:14.189 --> 00:07:18.050
• Documents were found describing Merck
employees working either independently or

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:23.199
in collaboration with medical publishing companies
to prepare manuscripts and subsequently recruiting

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:27.949
external academically affiliated investigators
to be authors.

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:32.030
• Recruited authors were frequently placed
in the first and second positions of the authorship

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:33.309
list.

00:07:33.309 --> 00:07:40.840
• The use of guest or honorary authorship
was an accepted practice at Merck apparently.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:46.080
They also looked at 72 review papers that
the company was involved in publishing.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:47.080
They found:

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:51.139
• Merck marketing employees developing plans
for manuscripts, contracting with medical

00:07:51.139 --> 00:07:56.319
publishing companies to ghostwrite manuscripts,
and recruiting external, academically affiliated

00:07:56.319 --> 00:07:59.199
investigators to be authors.

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:03.319
• Recruited authors were commonly the sole
author on the manuscript and offered honoraria

00:08:03.319 --> 00:08:05.689
for their participation.

00:08:05.689 --> 00:08:11.009
• Only half of the review articles even
disclosed that Merck had sponsored those papers.

00:08:11.009 --> 00:08:15.759
That's quite insidious and you can see that
this could have profound impact on the medical

00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:17.379
literature.

00:08:17.379 --> 00:08:21.379
Merck could actually put this whole set of
opinions and perspectives out in the medical

00:08:21.379 --> 00:08:25.740
literature with it having the stamp of coming
from an academic institution, when it really

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.939
came from the drug company marketing people.

00:08:28.939 --> 00:08:34.310
You can see why this has been such a hot issue.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:38.900
Just to give you an idea, this is an actual
internal company email that was made public

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.070
during the court trial.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:44.139
It shows you how blatant this practice is.

00:08:44.139 --> 00:08:46.730
The company writes to the potential guest
author:

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:52.310
“I would like to invite you to be an author
on the abstract and manuscript for this study.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:56.820
We are currently preparing both for submission
before the end of this year.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.529
Could you please let me know if you would
be interested in authorship on both the abstract

00:09:00.529 --> 00:09:04.880
and manuscript, one of the two planned publications,
or none?

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.820
In making your decision, you may want to take
into consideration that the results of this

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.860
study were negative at first glance.”

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.930
Basically, the company is giving out authorship
like a gift.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:19.570
And they're allowing the researcher to choose
whether or not they want to accept this gift

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:23.230
based on how the study results happened to
come out.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:27.399
Clearly the author being invited here has
no basis for taking public responsibility

00:09:27.399 --> 00:09:29.980
for the content of these articles.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:35.070
Obviously, this researcher was not heavily
involved in the analysis, because the analysis

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:40.130
is already done, or the writing of the paper,
it's already being drafted.

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:43.130
This is a clear case of guest authorship.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:47.150
And the trial turned up many emails like this
that made it obvious as to what was going

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:52.440
on.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.810
In summary, naming authors on a scientific
paper ensures that the appropriate individuals

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.420
get credit, and are accountable, for the research.

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:05.860
Deliberately misrepresenting a scientist's
relationship to their work is considered to

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:10.379
be a form of misconduct that undermines confidence
in the reporting of the work itself.

